= Social metabolism — Simple =

'''Social metabolism''' studies the materials and energy that flow through human societies.

A city, for example, needs food, water, fuel, electricity, building materials, and manufactured goods. Some of these materials become long-lasting stocks such as roads and buildings. Others leave as waste, wastewater, emissions, or exported products.

Researchers measure these flows to understand how societies physically depend on nature.

== Why call it metabolism? ==

Living organisms take in matter and energy, transform them, and release wastes.

Societies also have physical inputs, transformations, stocks, and outputs.

The comparison is useful because the flows are real and measurable. But society is not literally one biological organism.

== Material balance ==

For a material that is conserved, the basic accounting idea is:

<math>change\ in\ stock = inputs - outputs.</math>

If 100 tonnes of material enter a city and 75 tonnes leave during a year, about 25 tonnes must remain in buildings, products, infrastructure, or other stocks, assuming all flows were measured correctly.

== Material-flow accounting ==

Economy-wide material-flow accounts track materials entering and leaving national economies.

Eurostat records flows such as domestic extraction, imports, and exports under standardized environmental-economic accounting rules.<ref name="eurostat">[https://ec.europa.eu/eurostat/cache/metadata/EN/env_ac_mfa_simsmf_ie.htm Eurostat, “Material flow accounts.”]</ref>

A common measure is:

<math>DMC = domestic\ extraction + imports - exports.</math>

DMC means domestic material consumption.

It does not count every material extracted abroad to make imported products.

== Stocks matter ==

Buildings, roads, machines, vehicles, and other infrastructure can remain in use for decades.

These stocks provide services but also create future maintenance and replacement needs.

A growing city can therefore use enormous amounts of material even when household consumption seems modest.

== Energy metabolism ==

Societies also depend on energy.

Historical researchers often distinguish broad regimes:

* hunter-gatherer societies;
* agrarian societies using mainly biomass and solar energy;
* industrial societies using large amounts of fossil fuel and minerals.

These are simplified categories, not rigid stages every society follows in exactly the same way.

== Environmental impacts ==

Large material and energy flows can produce:

* greenhouse-gas emissions;
* mining impacts;
* land-use change;
* pollution;
* waste;
* biodiversity pressure.

But material quantity alone does not tell the whole story. One tonne of gravel and one tonne of toxic metal have very different impacts.

== Efficiency is not the same as using less overall ==

A country may use fewer materials for each dollar of economic output while total material use still rises.

So:

<pre>
better efficiency
does not automatically mean
lower total resource use
</pre>

Researchers distinguish relative improvements from absolute reductions.

== Other methods ==

Material-flow analysis tracks physical flows.

Life-cycle assessment studies environmental impacts associated with a product or service.

Input-output analysis studies connections among industries.

These tools can be combined, but they answer different questions.

== Social questions still matter ==

Physical accounting does not tell us who benefits or who is harmed.

A complete environmental analysis may also need to study inequality, labor, land rights, health, and political power.

